na U.S. Senate race", "source": "Elon University", "published": "2026-09-10T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiyAFBVV95cUxPaTFDQmRETUp3U3NTRDJPc0l6clJCQ0RraTM1cnlhOEstRmpnVm9nUkZSX1VHWlBEbUxzYk5UYmlfMUpOMjZDQ2tfMWNkM3gxc3VpU2hqNGpGcGpIZDNJby0yNFVmODZuSlpOVi1BeUJUaFVrdVdRWXpHSFFBdjg3em1MakJCQThIRXVxdDZveFJfby1LenpGSldmcWZfb0toSjBhdVJsY0xSdHphQkxQN2Z5bHVuLWU0ZWpDUG41THd1VTJuNFd3bg?oc=5"}, {"title": "North Carolina Senate race between Cooper, Whatley tightens: Poll", "source": "The Hill", "published": "2026-08-21T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 6.83, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 10.13, "poll_sd": 4.43, "poll_weight": 0.56, "fundamentals": {"margin": 6.94, "lean": -1.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.56}, "rating_margin": 6.75, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.84, "baseline_margin": 8.68, "analyst_adjustment": -0.68, "key_factors": ["Cooper's polling lead averages around 9-10 points across recent surveys, including Republican-leaning pollsters", "Cooper's fundraising dominance: $35M raised and $20.7M on hand versus Whatley's $11.3M", "Open seat with no incumbent on the ballot, in a near-even state lean", "Prediction markets price Cooper's win probability at 93-95%"], "rationale": "Recent polls consistently show Roy Cooper ahead by 5 to 12 points, with even Republican-sponsored surveys putting him up 5, and prediction markets price him near 94%. The fundamentals are somewhat less favorable than the polling, so the forecast settles at an 8-point Cooper margin — a clear Likely D flip of the open seat.", "watch": "Republican outside spending against Cooper ($8.8M already) and any late tightening among older voters, who polls show breaking for Whatley, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat pits Democrat Roy Cooper, a two-term governor, against Republican Michael Whatley, the former state GOP chair and national party official. With Thom Tillis retiring, neither side has incumbency advantages, and the state's near-even partisan lean would normally suggest a close contest.\n\nInstead, Cooper has led every recent poll, by margins ranging from 5 to 12 points, and has raised roughly three times Whatley's total. Expert raters unanimously call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt D to Likely D, and prediction markets put Cooper's chances above 90%. The race remains a Likely Democratic gain, with some room for late movement.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.93, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9375, "margin": 8.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9346, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9264, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9322, "margin": 8.0}]}